WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
A larva has lost part of its body pattern, but nobody knows the gene.

2
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.680
Which end of the problem should we start from?

3
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.680
Genetics can run from a visible phenotype toward a gene, or from a chosen gene

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.680
toward a phenotype.

5
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:20.063
Keep the laboratory bench in view.

6
00:00:21.363 --> 00:00:26.003
On its left, organisms with varied phenotypes move toward an assay gate.

7
00:00:27.303 --> 00:00:30.943
On its right, a named gene enters before any phenotype appears.

8
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:33.963
These are not different toolkits.

9
00:00:35.143 --> 00:00:36.923
They are different directions of reasoning.

10
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:42.667
In the phenotype-first lane, random mutations enter before the assay.

11
00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:46.427
The gate keeps individuals with the chosen trait.

12
00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:52.047
Researchers recover lines, map each lesion, and test a candidate gene.

13
00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:56.687
This forward path can uncover genes nobody expected.

14
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:00.007
But only visible outcomes reach the gate.

15
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.710
Now imagine a mutation that changes meiosis but never changes the trait scored at

16
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.210
the gate. Will this forward screen recover it?

17
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:15.850
Watch the unaffected-looking individuals pass beyond the assay while the hidden

18
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.790
mutation stays inside them.

19
00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:29.417
No. The screen cannot select a difference its assay never records.

20
00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:35.777
Forward genetics is open to unexpected mechanisms, yet bounded by phenotype.

21
00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:39.797
A different assay might reveal the same mutation.

22
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:44.237
The blind spot belongs to the measurement, not to the gene.

23
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.390
In the gene-first lane, begin with a named gene.

24
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.610
Lower its output with RNA interference, or alter its DNA with Cas9.

25
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.110
Then measure what changes.

26
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.910
This reverse path asks a focused question.

27
00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.370
It stays silent about genes nobody chose to perturb.

28
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.920
The approaches therefore miss different things.

29
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.840
Forward screens miss phenotypes outside their assay.

30
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.520
Reverse experiments miss genes outside their chosen list.

31
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.940
Neither direction turns one changed trait into a complete mechanism.

32
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.220
That requires a separate causal test.

33
00:02:27.183 --> 00:02:30.063
A pooled Cas9 screen combines the directions.

34
00:02:31.543 --> 00:02:36.103
Researchers choose many guides gene by gene, then usually give each cell a single

35
00:02:36.103 --> 00:02:39.703
guide. A selection keeps cells with a phenotype.

36
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:44.803
Sequencing identifies guides that became enriched or depleted.

37
00:02:46.123 --> 00:02:48.143
Those guide counts nominate candidates.

38
00:02:49.217 --> 00:02:51.337
Follow the bench from question to claim.

39
00:02:52.597 --> 00:02:56.037
A phenotype-first screen may discover an unimagined gene.

40
00:02:57.337 --> 00:03:00.737
A gene-first experiment may test a precise hypothesis.

41
00:03:02.217 --> 00:03:05.077
A pooled screen may survey many chosen genes together.

42
00:03:06.397 --> 00:03:09.677
In every case, the assay defines what becomes visible.

43
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.900
So ask these questions before interpreting any genetic result.

44
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.740
What did the experiment choose first?

45
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.860
What could its assay actually detect?

46
00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.500
Those questions reveal both its reach and its blind spot.

47
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.740
The direction is part of the evidence.
